<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 317 0 R
/E (Before we take a deep dive into the biochemical processes that occur daily in your local bakery, we need to understand a few key concepts of molecular biology. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
518 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 317 0 R
/E (Biomolecules are organic compounds, which means they are synthesised by organisms, made up of at least one carbon atom linked to at least one hydrogen atom through a covalent bond. Macromolecules, biomolecules composed of a large number of atoms, often in the order of the millions, can be classified into four main groups, but we will focus on the two most important for breadbaking: carbohydrates and proteins.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
519 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 317 0 R
/E (Carbohydrates are composed of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen \(C, H, O\). Most carbohydrates are very complex and are composed of linkages between smaller molecules: monosaccharides. Saccharose \(the carbohydrate present in refined sugar\), for example, results from the link between glucose and fructose, two monosaccharides.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
520 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 317 0 R
/E (Now, onto proteins! Proteins are extremely complex biomolecules, whose main elements are carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen. They are composed of amino acids, small molecules capable of adhering to only two other amino acids, which results in a linear sequence, which, when it achieves a large size, forms proteins. What makes proteins so interesting is that these linear sequences of amino acids can fold over themselves \(and each other!\) in growingly complex order.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
