/P 319 0 R
/E (Now comes the cool stuff: yeast! Remember all the starch that flour was made of? Well, starch is a polysaccharide, also known as a carbohydrate. It is, however, a very complex carbohydrate, which means the S. cerevisiae, a unicellular fungus, is unable to absorb it. Therefore, they add secress two types of enzymes \(proteins that accelerate chemical reactions\) to our dough in order to externally digest these sugars and break them down to simpler ones. The first one is amylase that decomposes starch into maltose, and then maltase, which breaks down this maltose to glucose. The fungi then feed off of this glucose in order to attain energy, releasing carbon dioxide and ethanol \(an alcohol\) in the process through the following chemical equation:)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
